BT
/F1 10 Tf
14 TL
45 800 Td
(BAB 3 Turning Experience into Shared Knowledge) Tj
T*
(Individual learning becomes valuable to an organisation only when it can be shared.) Tj
T*
(Sharing is not the same as telling. A person who explains a lesson in a meeting may feel) Tj
T*
(heard, but the lesson may not survive the meeting. Durable sharing requires a form that) Tj
T*
(other people can find and use later: a short note, a checklist, a documented example, or) Tj
T*
(a recorded decision with its reasoning.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Documentation has a reputation for being heavy and outdated. That reputation comes from) Tj
T*
(documents that try to capture everything. A more useful approach is to capture the) Tj
T*
(smallest piece of knowledge that changes behaviour. A single paragraph explaining why a) Tj
T*
(common fix causes a problem can save hours. A checklist with five items can prevent a) Tj
T*
(recurring mistake. Small documents are more likely to be read and updated.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(A community of practice can accelerate sharing, but only if it has a purpose beyond) Tj
T*
(meetings. The purpose might be to improve a specific process, to review difficult cases,) Tj
T*
(or to maintain a shared standard. Without a purpose, the community becomes a social) Tj
T*
(calendar. With a purpose, it creates artefacts that other people can use. The artefacts) Tj
T*
(are the evidence that the community is working.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Mentoring is another form of shared knowledge, but it depends on a relationship rather) Tj
T*
(than a document. A mentor can explain not only what to do but why it matters in a) Tj
T*
(particular context. This kind of explanation is difficult to capture in a manual. It) Tj
T*
(requires time and trust. A mentoring relationship works best when both people have a) Tj
T*
(clear goal and a regular opportunity to review progress.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Knowledge also disappears when people leave. A team that depends on one person for a) Tj
T*
(critical process is exposed, even if that person is generous. Reducing the dependency is) Tj
T*
(not a sign of distrust. It is a form of respect for the person and for the team. The) Tj
T*
(work is to make the critical knowledge explicit while the person is still available to) Tj
T*
(explain it.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(None of these practices requires a large programme. A team can begin with one small) Tj
T*
(habit: after a difficult task, write five lines about what changed and where the note) Tj
T*
(can be found. Over time, the notes form a record of experience that new people can read) Tj
T*
(and experienced people can challenge. This record is not a substitute for practice, but) Tj
T*
(it makes practice easier to repeat.) Tj
T*
() Tj
ET
endstream
